r+=['','The freshly paired official 2024–25 NBA logs produce 54.47% home wins (670/1,230), compared with the earlier audit reference of 54.39%. The earlier baseline files were not altered. This small reference discrepancy is exposed rather than silently rewriting the previous report.','', 'Sources: https://stats.nba.com/stats/leaguegamelog and https://stats.wnba.com/stats/leaguegamelog; complete query parameters are embedded in each retained response. SeasonType=Regular Season, PlayerOrTeam=T, LeagueID=00/10. Additional raw reference files and fetch status are in dev/phase4/. Original official reference logs remain in dev/phase3/.','', '## 1. Team strength: confirmed prediction weakness, not a possession multiplier','', 'dist/game.js:49 defines strength as 80% first-five OVR plus 20% the remaining-roster OVR average. It includes injured roster members and ignores minutes, defensive/offensive specialties, stamina and positional coverage. That can misclassify comparable teams. It is not used as a global bonus in the possession engine.','', 'Consumers traced: schedule-spreads.js (pregame spread); schedule-ui.js (projected schedule difficulty); power-rankings.js; playoff-field.js, playoff-series.js and playoffs.js (win/series forecasts); dashboard.js and game.js (display); trade-logic.js (unlocked future-pick valuation); gm-career.js (job requirements, expectations and management evaluation). The local strength variable in statistical-balance.js is a separate rebounding calculation, not this OVR function. No direct home-court, scoring or schedule-matchmaking multiplier was found from this global OVR function. Its AI valuation uses can have indirect long-term effects.','', '## 2. Rotation-aware diagnostic model','', 'dev/rotation-quality-diagnostic.js projects the existing rotation for a full regulation game on a copied club. It reuses availability, depth, minute targets, workload caps, stamina depletion/recovery and coaching. It measures minutes-weighted effective offensive/defensive attributes, rebounding, passing, ball handling, energy, bench share and coaching skill/fit. The projection never changes individual possession calculations.','', 'It is approximate: minute-granularity rotation checks, a neutral score margin, no future fouls/ejections, injuries during play or garbage time, and no game-specific altitude context. It does not claim to forecast every eventual substitution. Availability tests exclude injured players and verify 240 NBA/200 WNBA player-minutes; the fatigued-player fixture changes the projection. RNG and state immutability are asserted. There is no separate persistent suspension mechanism in the audited availability code, so this phase does not invent one.','', 'The quality-model experiment uses preserved Phase 3 mechanics to isolate prediction quality. It is not a fitted Phase 4 spread model. Fitting uses seeds 201–204; evaluation uses separate seeds 205–208. Each league has 800 training and 800 held-out games (3,200 total). Every fourth observation deliberately makes one home starter unavailable; this is a stress test, not a claim about real injury prevalence. The ridge penalty is fixed at 20. A fitted version of the old OVR estimate is included to distinguish feature improvements from merely fitting an intercept/slope.','', '| League / held-out model | Games | MAE | RMSE | Signed bias (prediction minus actual) | Brier score |','|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
for l,v in qe.items():
 for model,m in v['heldOut'].items():r.append(f"| {l} / {model} | {m['n']} | {m['MAE']:.3f} | {m['RMSE']:.3f} | {m['bias']:+.3f} | {m['brier']:.4f} |")
